<%#- ESCAPING: <%% emits runtime ERB into the generated view; a bare <% is a
     generation-time loop over the schema. The #{...} inside an escaped tag is
     runtime Ruby and passes through untouched. -%>
<%%# locals: (<%= singular_name %>:, actions: true, editing: false, form: nil<%= schema.belongs_tos.map { ", #{it.options_local}: []" }.join %>) -%>
<%%# `<%= singular_name %>` is duck-typed: the row projection from the index and
    from the channel's render effect, the ActiveRecord model from the show page.
    Both answer the column readers plus the association labels (the row carries
    them, the model delegates them), so this partial has one contract and two
    satisfying types.

    It reads NO controller context. A channel renders it through
    ApplicationController.render, whose controller instance never ran #process:
    action_name is nil, params and session are empty, and controller_name is
    "application" — all SILENTLY. Anything this partial branches on arrives as
    a local with a default instead. %>
<%%# The root id is OUTSIDE the display/edit branch on purpose: a stable id
    across both modes is what lets idiomorph pair the subtrees under
    broadcast_morph instead of rebuilding them (and losing focus). %>
<div id="<%= row_dom_id_prefix %>_<%%= <%= singular_name %>.id %>"<%= css_attr(:card) %>>
  <div<%= css_attr(:card_body) %>>
    <%% if editing %>
    <%%= render "<%= partial_path(row_form_partial) %>", <%= wrapped_list(row_form_render_locals, indent: render_indent(partial_path(row_form_partial))).lstrip %> %>
    <%% else %>
<% schema.columns.each do |column| -%>
    <div>
      <strong><%= column.human_name %>:</strong>
<% if column.type == :boolean -%>
      <%% if actions %>
        <%%# A checkbox, because a changed control reports its CHECKED state
            rather than its value attribute: the client sends a JSON boolean
            under the control's name, so the server sets the value it was given
            instead of flipping whatever it finds — which is also what makes
            two tabs racing on one row converge.

            No hidden-field pair: that convention exists for form SUBMIT, and
            this control talks to the channel directly. %>
        <%%= check_box_tag <%= wrapped_list(row_toggle_args(column), indent: 26).lstrip %> %>
      <%% else %>
        <%%= <%= singular_name %>.<%= column.name %> ? "Yes" : "No" %>
      <%% end %>
<% else -%>
      <%%= <%= singular_name %>.<%= column.display_reader %> %>
<% end -%>
    </div>

<% end -%>
    <%% if actions %>
    <div<%= css_attr(:card_actions) %>>
      <%%= link_to "Show this <%= human_name.downcase %>", <%= singular_route_name %>_path(<%= singular_name %>.id)<%= css? ? %(, class: "#{css(:btn_outline)}") : "" %> %>
      <%%= tag.button(<%= wrapped_list(row_button_args("Edit", :edit, :btn_outline), indent: 21).lstrip %>) %>
      <%%# Per-row pending feedback costs nothing at this site and there is
          nothing here to see: the client stamps data-hibiki-busy on the
          control that fired, so the transport stylesheet dims THIS row's
          button and no other while its trip is in flight. No server state, no
          markup, no per-row bookkeeping. Destroy is also the site where the
          feedback matters most — it is the one gesture nobody wants to repeat
          because the first click looked like it did nothing. %>
      <%%# The scaffold's button_to carried data-turbo-confirm, which does
          nothing on a hibiki-driven button — it is not a Turbo-driven form.
          confirm: is the equivalent, gated client-side before the action is
          ever performed. %>
      <%%= tag.button(<%= wrapped_list(row_button_args("Destroy", :destroy, :btn_warning, confirm: "Are you sure?"), indent: 21).lstrip %>) %>
    </div>
    <%% end %>
    <%% end %>
  </div>
</div>
